"An orchestra has four main sections.": "2d74243ea6a5",
"An orchestra is a large instrumental ensemble that combines instruments from different families. There are typically four main sections: strings, woodwinds, brass, and percussion. A full-size orchestra may be called a symphony orchestra or a philharmonic orchestra, and Taiwan’s dictionary says there is no real difference between the two names. Its size may vary from seventy to over one hundred players, depending on the music and the hall.": "5464ab339950",
"An orchestra is a large instrumental ensemble that combines instruments from different families. There are typically four main sections: strings, woodwinds, brass, and percussion. A full-size orchestra may be called a symphony orchestra or a philharmonic orchestra, and Taiwan’s dictionary says there is no real difference between the two names. Its size may vary from seventy to over one hundred players, depending on the music and the hall. The strings are the first and second violins, violas, cellos, and double basses, and they are usually the most numerous group. The woodwind section usually includes flutes, oboes, clarinets, and bassoons. The brass are French horns, trumpets, trombones, and a tuba. The percussion include the timpani, snare drum, bass drum, cymbals, and triangle. The timpani can be tuned to particular notes. A harp is often added, and keyboard instruments may form a fifth section. These four sections are not the same as the four families in lesson 9. The woodwinds and the brass are both aerophones, because air vibrates in both. The percussion section includes membranophones, such as the timpani, and idiophones, such as the cymbals. A piano is a chordophone. And the names do not describe materials: a saxophone is usually made of brass but belongs to the woodwinds, and a brass instrument is defined by the way its sound is made. In the usual plan the strings sit at the front, in a semicircle around the conductor’s podium. The Berlin Philharmonic describes one general principle: the sections are arranged by volume. The softer strings sit at the front, then the more powerful woodwinds, then the louder brass, and finally the percussion at the back. But it is a tradition and not a rule. The conductors decide, thinking about the music, the hall, how well the sections blend, and whether they can see the players. Even the strings can be arranged in different ways. In the so-called American setup, the first violins, second violins, violas, and cellos sit in that order from the audience’s left. In the German setup, the first and second violins sit opposite each other, with the cellos and violas between them. The double basses sit behind the strings, on the left or the right. Even the horns may switch sides. So the picture in this lesson is one possible plan. The conductor stands on a raised podium. The conductor sets the tempo, makes sure each group comes in at the right time, and shapes the phrasing, often with a baton. Most conductors do not play an instrument when they conduct, so all the sound is made by the musicians. The concertmaster, who leads the first violins, is the second-in-command and leads the tuning. By a convention hundreds of years old, the first oboe plays the note A for the orchestra to tune to.": "58cbfcc807f8",
"Even the strings can be arranged in different ways. In the so-called American setup, the first violins, second violins, violas, and cellos sit in that order from the audience’s left. In the German setup, the first and second violins sit opposite each other, with the cellos and violas between them. The double basses sit behind the strings, on the left or the right. Even the horns may switch sides. So the picture in this lesson is one possible plan.": "cbb32a2d5fbf",
